<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p><strong>Self-study quiz.</strong> This quiz is for your own review — answers are not recorded. You’ll get instant feedback on each question.</p>
</div></div>

<div class="quiz"><div class="quiz__top-bar"><div class="quiz__score-tracker" style="display:none;"></div><button class="quiz__start-over" type="button" style="display:none;">Start Over</button></div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q1b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">1</span> Which list gives Aristotle's six elements of drama?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1a" id="q1a">
        <label for="q1a">Comedy, tragedy, farce, melodrama, satire, and romance.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1b" id="q1b">
        <label for="q1b">Plot, character, diction, thought, spectacle, and music.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1c" id="q1c">
        <label for="q1c">Beginning, middle, end, prologue, epilogue, and chorus.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1d" id="q1d">
        <label for="q1d">Actor, director, stage, costume, script, and audience.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. These six categories still apply: when you analyze a play, you are identifying how they work together.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Aristotle's six elements concern the parts of a play: what happens, who, how they speak, what ideas, what we see, and what we hear.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q2a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">2</span> For Aristotle, plot is</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2a" id="q2a">
        <label for="q2a">the structured arrangement of events that produces rising action, a climax, falling action, and a resolution.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2b" id="q2b">
        <label for="q2b">simply what happens, in any order.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2c" id="q2c">
        <label for="q2c">the actors' personalities.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2d" id="q2d">
        <label for="q2d">the costumes and scenery.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The arrangement matters because it determines the emotional arc of the audience's experience.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Aristotle does not mean merely what happens. He means how events are arranged to build toward a climax and a resolution.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q3d">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">3</span> A tragedy follows a protagonist who</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3a" id="q3a">
        <label for="q3a">never changes.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3b" id="q3b">
        <label for="q3b">moves from disorder to order and marries.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3c" id="q3c">
        <label for="q3c">begins in poverty and ends as a king.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3d" id="q3d">
        <label for="q3d">is of high standing and falls from fortune to misfortune because of a flaw or error in judgment.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The flaw is called hamartia. Oedipus's is his relentless commitment to knowing the truth.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Tragedy moves downward, from fortune to misfortune, because of a flaw or mistake. Comedy moves the other way.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q4c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">4</span> What is catharsis?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4a" id="q4a">
        <label for="q4a">The use of music in a play.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4b" id="q4b">
        <label for="q4b">A character's long speech to the audience.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4c" id="q4c">
        <label for="q4c">The release of the pity and fear that tragedy arouses in the audience.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4d" id="q4d">
        <label for="q4d">The final scene of a comedy.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Aristotle thought tragedy produced pity for the character's suffering and fear that we might make the same mistake, followed by a release of those feelings.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Catharsis is a purging. The emotions released are pity and fear.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q5a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">5</span> Comedy typically moves</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5a" id="q5a">
        <label for="q5a">from disorder to order, often ending in marriages, reconciliations, or celebrations.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5b" id="q5b">
        <label for="q5b">from order to disorder and ends in death.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5c" id="q5c">
        <label for="q5c">from the audience to the stage.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5d" id="q5d">
        <label for="q5d">from a king's rise to his exile.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Comedy moves from confusion to clarity and from isolation to community. The lesson links it to spring, a season of renewal.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Comedy moves in the opposite direction from tragedy: toward order, community, and celebration.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q6b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">6</span> What is a soliloquy?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6a" id="q6a">
        <label for="q6a">A conversation between two characters.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6b" id="q6b">
        <label for="q6b">A speech by a character who is alone, giving the audience access to inner thought that other characters do not hear.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6c" id="q6c">
        <label for="q6c">A silent scene with only music.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6d" id="q6d">
        <label for="q6d">A chorus of many voices.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. A play cannot narrate a character's thoughts the way a novel can, so the soliloquy is the main way to hear them.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Solo is the root. A soliloquy is a character speaking alone, so the audience hears what others in the play do not.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q7c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">7</span> In Sophocles' Oedipus Rex, Oedipus's hamartia is</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7a" id="q7a">
        <label for="q7a">a plan to overthrow the gods.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7b" id="q7b">
        <label for="q7b">his laziness as a king.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7c" id="q7c">
        <label for="q7c">his relentless commitment to discovering the truth, a quality admirable in every other context.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7d" id="q7d">
        <label for="q7d">his dislike of the city of Thebes.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The hamartia is not a moral failing but a refusal to stop knowing, and that is what makes the play tragic rather than merely unfortunate.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The lesson stresses that the flaw is not evil. It is a virtue taken to its limit: the determination to learn the truth.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q8a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">8</span> In Death of a Salesman, Willy Loman's hamartia is</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8a" id="q8a">
        <label for="q8a">his refusal to see reality, clinging to a fantasy version of the American Dream.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8b" id="q8b">
        <label for="q8b">his decision to leave the city.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8c" id="q8c">
        <label for="q8c">his talent as a salesman.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8d" id="q8d">
        <label for="q8d">his devotion to his sons.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Miller argues that an ordinary person's failure deserves the same attention as a king's fall.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The lesson says Willy's flaw is the opposite of Oedipus's: he will not look at reality.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q9b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">9</span> How does Hamilton use diction to shape the content of its story?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9a" id="q9a">
        <label for="q9a">It uses no language at all.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9b" id="q9b">
        <label for="q9b">Hip-hop gives Hamilton's relentless ambition, speed of thought, and need to be heard a form that matches them.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9c" id="q9c">
        <label for="q9c">Its dialogue is written in Shakespearean verse.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9d" id="q9d">
        <label for="q9d">Its diction is irrelevant to the story.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The language is inseparable from the content: the form of Miranda's lyrics is the form of Hamilton's mind.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The point of the lesson is that diction reveals the world of the play. Here the choice of hip-hop is a deliberate expression of character.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q10d">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">10</span> On a thrust stage, the audience sits</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10a" id="q10a">
        <label for="q10a">entirely around the stage.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10b" id="q10b">
        <label for="q10b">far above the stage.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10c" id="q10c">
        <label for="q10c">on one side only, looking through a frame.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10d" id="q10d">
        <label for="q10d">on three sides of the stage, which makes scenes feel more intimate.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. A proscenium theater has the audience on one side, a thrust stage on three, and a theater in the round on all sides. Shakespeare's Globe was a thrust stage.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Think of the stage pushed out into the audience. A thrust stage has audience on three sides.</div>
  </div>
</div>

